uct of diagonal entries?","options":["At each expansion step, the off-diagonal zero terms vanish.","Every cofactor of a diagonal matrix equals a diagonal entry.","Each step deletes a zero row, so the matrix shrinks by one.","Every minor of a diagonal matrix is equal to $1$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"abcaf65c23d7d897ee547786","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch9_cofactor_expansion_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Expand $\\det\\begin{pmatrix}1&2&3\\\\0&4&0\\\\5&6&7\\end{pmatrix}$ along the second row. Which expression is correct?","options":["$4\\det\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\5&6\\end{pmatrix}$","$-4\\det\\begin{pmatrix}1&3\\\\5&7\\end{pmatrix}$","$4\\det\\begin{pmatrix}1&3\\\\5&7\\end{pmatrix}$","$4\\det\\begin{pmatrix}2&3\\\\6&7\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2422bb8b0733eaaf91cb6c46","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch9_cofactor_expansion_007_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Expand along the first row to compute $\\det\\begin{pmatrix}0&2&0\\\\1&3&4\\\\5&6&7\\end{pmatrix}$.","options":["$-26$","$6$","$26$","$18$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9d30e4644d9514955057a2bc","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch9_cofactor_expansion_010_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A $3\\times3$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{A}$ has $a_{12}=-3$. Which expression equals the cofactor $C_{12}$?","options":["$\\det(\\boldsymbol{A}_{12})$","$-3\\det(\\boldsymbol{A}_{12})$","$3\\det(\\boldsymbol{A}_{12})$","$-\\det(\\boldsymbol{A}_{12})$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3aa2093ad82f7877d550ea75","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch9_cofactor_expansion_018_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Two students expand $\\det(\\boldsymbol{A})$ for the same $3\\times3$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{A}$: one along row $1$ gets $7$, the other along column $3$ gets $-7$. What follows?","options":["At least one of them has made an error.","Both can be right: column expansion gives $-\\det(\\boldsymbol{A})$.","Both can be right: the result depends on the row or column used.","Both can be right, because $\\boldsymbol{A}$ need not be symmetric."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8ed380038b7f9fc78617bc10","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_cofactor_expansion_d582c688_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{A}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2&3\\\\2&5&6\\\\0&1&5\\end{pmatrix}$. Subtracting $2$ times row $1$ from row $2$ turns row $2$ into $(0,1,0)$ and leaves the other rows unchanged. Using this row operation and a cofactor expansion along the new row $2$, what is $\\det(\\boldsymbol{A})$?","options":["$-5$","$25$","$5$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6f5748ab072cb952135f2f53","time_limit":35}]}}